    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Можно тебя на минуточку?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can I have a word with you for a minute?' In Russian, 'may' leads into 'a minute' in a very natural everyday phrasing. And Kristina jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am listening.' The first content word means 'I', and 'I'm listening' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Ты много сделала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have done a lot.' The key word here means 'you', which Kristina combines with 'a lot'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Но это прошлое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But this is the past.' You'll hear 'this' all the time in the show, here connected with 'last'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Нам нужны новые люди.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need new people.' The key word here means 'us', which Kristina combines with 'needed'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Новая кровь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'New blood.' Kristina starts with 'new' and pairs it with 'blood' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы меня увольняете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you firing me?' Vika starts with 'you' and pairs it with 'me' to make the point clear. Then Kristina replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Да.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes.' Here 'yes' sets up the idea, while 'yes' comes at the end for emphasis.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я надеюсь, ты найдешь работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope you will find a job.' Listen for the word meaning 'I' right alongside 'hope so' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не волнуйтесь за меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not worry about me.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'worry' turns the action back onto the subject. And Kristina jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Хорошо, не буду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, I won't.' Listen for the word meaning 'good' right alongside 'will be' in this line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "До свидания.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Goodbye.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'meeting' straight to 'meeting'. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
